// ---- Quantized vortex lattice in a rotating superfluid (Abrikosov lattice) ---------------
// A superfluid cannot rotate like a solid body. The only way it can carry angular momentum is
// to punch an array of vortices through itself, and their circulation is QUANTIZED — each is
// a phase defect where the density goes to exactly zero and the phase winds by exactly 2*pi.
// They then arrange into the triangular lattice Abrikosov predicted.
//
// The order parameter is built from the vortex positions:
//   |psi|^2 = prod_k r_k^2/(r_k^2 + xi^2)      (a core hole per vortex, xi = healing length)
//   arg psi = sum_k arg(z - z_k)
// Every vortex here has the SAME sign (that is what rotation means), which is what makes the
// phase cheap: sum of args == arg of the product, so each pixel accumulates ONE complex
// product and takes ONE atan2 at the end instead of N of them.
//
// Dynamics are honest 2D point-vortex: each vortex is advected by the others. A same-sign
// cluster conserves its second moment, so it needs no trap — it rotates as a body and rings
// with undamped Tkachenko modes (the soft transverse oscillations of the lattice itself).
// The condensate phase also advances at the chemical potential, e^(-i*mu*t), which sweeps the
